<Project>

  <!-- ====================================================================================
       Versioning — ONE place for every project in the repository.

       WHAT WAS BROKEN. Both shipped csprojs hardcoded three separate version properties:
       <AssemblyVersion>2.0.1</AssemblyVersion>, <FileVersion>2.0.1</FileVersion> and
       <PackageVersion>2.0.2</PackageVersion>. Command-line properties cannot override a
       value the project sets unconditionally, so the release workflow's
       `-p:Version=$TAG` was silently discarded for packaging:

           $ dotnet pack src/JsonSettings/JsonSettings.csproj -p:Version=2.1.0
           -> Nucs.JsonSettings.2.0.2.nupkg

       Tagging v2.1.0 would therefore have tried to publish 2.0.2, which is already on
       nuget.org, and been rejected with a 409. Worse, a single artifact carried three
       disagreeing versions at once: AssemblyVersion 2.1.0.0 (the workflow passed an EMPTY
       -p:AssemblyVersion=, which blanked the project value and let the SDK fall back to
       $(Version)), FileVersion 2.0.1 (project value, never overridden) and PackageVersion
       2.0.2 (project value, never overridden).

       WHAT REPLACES IT. Nothing below is unconditional, so anything passed on the command
       line wins, and $(VersionPrefix) is derived FROM $(Version) when only $(Version) is
       supplied. That makes a single `-p:Version=<tag>` correct on its own:

           -p:Version=2.1.0         -> Assembly 2.1.0, File 2.1.0, Package 2.1.0
           -p:Version=2.1.0-beta1   -> Assembly 2.1.0, File 2.1.0, Package 2.1.0-beta1

       The prerelease suffix is dropped from AssemblyVersion/FileVersion deliberately: the
       CLR binds on AssemblyVersion, so 2.1.0-beta1 and 2.1.0 must be the same identity or
       every consumer needs a binding redirect to move between them. That is the same rule
       the old workflow expressed as `ASSEMBLY_VERSION=${VERSION%%-*}` in shell — except
       that computed value was written to an output the packaging job never set, and so
       always expanded to the empty string.

       BUMPING THE VERSION FOR A LOCAL/DEV BUILD: edit the fallback below. Releases do not
       read it — they are driven entirely by the tag.
       ==================================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Split an explicit $(Version) into prefix + suffix so AssemblyVersion/FileVersion
         (which the SDK derives from $(VersionPrefix)) never carry a prerelease tag. -->
    <VersionPrefix Condition="'$(VersionPrefix)' == '' And '$(Version)' != '' And $(Version.Contains('-'))">$(Version.Substring(0, $(Version.IndexOf('-'))))</VersionPrefix>
    <VersionPrefix Condition="'$(VersionPrefix)' == '' And '$(Version)' != ''">$(Version)</VersionPrefix>

    <!-- Fallback for developer builds, `dotnet pack` with no arguments, and anyone who
         builds from a source archive rather than a tag. -->
    <VersionPrefix Condition="'$(VersionPrefix)' == ''">2.1.0</VersionPrefix>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ====================================================================================
       Shared package identity.

       These were duplicated verbatim across JsonSettings.csproj and
       JsonSettings.Autosave.csproj — including a 19-line <PackageReleaseNotes> block
       repeated character for character. Two copies of the same text drift; the copyright
       year already read 2023 in both shipped projects and 2021 in the Examples project.

       A project's own value still wins: Directory.Build.props is imported BEFORE the
       project body, so anything a csproj sets afterwards overrides what is here. That is
       how PackageId, Title, Description and Summary stay per-package.
       ==================================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>Nucs / Eli Belash</Authors>
    <Company>Nucs / Eli Belash</Company>
    <Copyright>Eli Belash @ 2026</Copyright>
    <NeutralLanguage />
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Nucs/JsonSettings</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Nucs/JsonSettings</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>nucs config file newtonsoft settings json configuration preferences</PackageTags>
    <PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>true</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
    <SignAssembly>false</SignAssembly>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ====================================================================================
       Package presentation.

       `dotnet pack` used to emit two deprecation warnings on every run:

         NU5125  the 'licenseUrl' element will be deprecated; use 'license'
         NU5048  'PackageIconUrl'/'iconUrl' is deprecated; use 'PackageIcon'/'icon'

       Both are more than cosmetic. nuget.org stopped rendering iconUrl, so the packages
       showed the default placeholder image, and licenseUrl pointed at a GitHub blob URL
       that no tooling can parse into a license identity, so `dotnet list package` with
       transitive output and every license-compliance scanner saw "unknown". The LICENSE
       in this repository is MIT, so the SPDX expression states that in the form tools read.

       The README was also declared but never shipped: JsonSettings.csproj had a
       <None Include="..\..\README.md"> with only a <Link>, which puts the file in the IDE
       solution tree and nowhere else. Packing it is what makes the nuget.org landing page
       show anything other than the one-line <Description>.
       ==================================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'false'">
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)assets\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- ====================================================================================
       Symbols and Source Link.

       Both shipped projects carried
       <AllowedOutputExtensionsInPackageBuildOutputFolder>...;.pdb</...>, which drops the
       .pdb next to the .dll inside lib/. That does make stepping possible, but it inflates
       every consumer's restore with debug data they did not ask for; across five targets
       it roughly doubled the package, and it is not what any debugger looks for.

       A .snupkg is the supported channel: nuget.org serves it from its symbol server, so
       Visual Studio and Rider fetch symbols on demand rather than everyone paying for them
       up front. Combined with Source Link the pdb also records where the sources live, so
       stepping into the library resolves to the exact commit on GitHub.

       ContinuousIntegrationBuild is scoped to CI on purpose: it normalises source paths to
       deterministic ones, which is what makes a rebuild of the same commit produce a
       byte-identical assembly, but it also makes local debugging harder to no benefit.
       ==================================================================================== -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>2.1.0
- Multi-targeting: netstandard2.0, net48, net6.0, net8.0 and net10.0 for both
  Nucs.JsonSettings and Nucs.JsonSettings.Autosave (Autosave previously shipped
  netstandard2.0 only).
- Removed the System.Security.SecureString 4.3.0 dependency. Every type used is part of
  netstandard2.0 and in-box on .NET Framework and modern .NET, so it only added a
  dependency edge.
- Upgraded Newtonsoft.Json to 13.0.3 and Castle.Core to 5.1.1.
- Packages now ship the README, declare their license as an SPDX expression instead of a
  deprecated licenseUrl, and publish a .snupkg symbol package with Source Link instead of
  embedding .pdb files in lib/.
- Paths: MoveFileEx is no longer P/Invoked on non-Windows, IsDirectoryWritable builds its
  probe path with Path.Combine, and Paths.ExecutingExe is no longer left null when the
  executable path cannot be determined (single-file publish).

2.0.2
- Added RecoveryModule and .WithRecovery to capture parsing errors
- Added JsonSettings.SuspendAutosave() extension
- Added proxy construction guard to prevent unncessary allocation on proxy creation.
- Fix non virtual built-in properties returning null after being wrapped with proxy.
- Added ProxyGeneratedAttribute that is attached to every proxy-generated class
- Module: made Dispose() inheritable.
- Module.Socket: changed to WeakReference&lt;JsonSettings&gt;
- SettingsBag: added support for SuspendAutosave #26
- VersioningModule: some internal fixes and renames #19
- Added RecoveryModule and WithRecovery api. #19
- JsonSettings: added TryingRecover and Recovered events.
- JsonSettings: Removed ThrowOnEmptyFile, this is handled by RecoveryModule now
- Made all module functions and variables protected or virtual.
- FluentJsonSettings: replaced dynamic creation with hardcoded creation (faster).
- Added proper Versioning example
- JsonSettings: add OnXXXX methods that trigger event changed to protected internal.
- Made all modules extensible
- SettingsBag: removed locking, optimized and replaced with ConcurrentDictionary for threadsafety
- Added documentation</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
